From 9b834e7d803df002ce62851b91c9f06aaa91e381 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Passerelle contributors <passerelle@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 01:51:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] virtio-pci: a modern PCI transport, behind transport.h

fs-9p reached only the virtio-9p devices QEMU puts on the `virt` machine's
fixed MMIO window, which is how an aarch64 guest sees them. An x86_64
guest normally gets its 9P share from `-virtfs`, which creates a
virtio-9p-pci device on the PCI bus, and that device is invisible to a
virtio-mmio driver. This adds the PCI transport, behind the existing
transport.h interface: client.c and the resource manager are untouched and
do not know which transport they got.

Only discovery, register access, notification and interrupts differ from
transport_virtio.c; the feature negotiation, the status sequence, the
split ring, the DMA memory, the interrupt service thread and the memory
barriers are the ones already proven on ARM64.

  src/virtio_pci_caps.{c,h}   capability decoding, no QNX header at all
  src/virtio_pci_int.h        private state, split from the public header
  src/transport_virtio_pci.c  PCI discovery, BARs, MSI-X, features, queue
  src/virtio_pci_ring.c       ring, request slots, interrupt thread

The decoder takes a config-space read callback instead of touching a bus,
so it is unit-tested on the host like lib9p:
tests/unit/test_virtio_pci_caps.c covers the capability-chain walk, a
cyclic chain, a device without NOTIFY, the fallback to a 32-bit-only
sweep, the rule that skips a capability's interior, the notification
address arithmetic and the queue-size clamp. Two scans exist because
pci_device_cfg_rd8() has been reported to return 0 silently at unaligned
offsets on QNX.

Two details come from observation rather than from a document, and are
commented as such:

  - pci_device_attach() does not enable bus mastering. Without the
    command register's memory-space and bus-master bits the device cannot
    DMA into the ring, so both are set explicitly.

  - With DO_BUS_CONFIG=no the QNX PCI server allocates no MSI vectors and
    pci_device_cfg_cap_enable() fails. MSI-X is therefore programmed by
    hand: an IRQ is claimed from the kernel's MSI range through
    rsrcdbmgr, table entry 0 is written through the BAR, and the command
    register's INTx-disable bit is set. When any step fails the transport
    falls back to the firmware's INTx line, which the virtio-mmio driver
    already exercises.

Completion is decided from the used ring, never from the wakeup, and a
request that times out marks its slot stale so QEMU can still finish
writing into buffers the driver has not reused.

Not verified on an x86_64 guest yet; a later patch says what to check.
